Reversal of Singly Linked List (3-Pointer In-Place Algorithm - O(n) Time, O(1) Space):
Uses three pointers `prev = NULL`, `curr = head`, `next = NULL`:
```c
struct Node* reverseList(struct Node* head) {
    struct Node *prev = NULL, *curr = head, *next = NULL;
    while (curr != NULL) {
        next = curr->next; // Store next node
        curr->next = prev; // Reverse pointer
        prev = curr;       // Advance prev
        curr = next;       // Advance curr
    }
    return prev; // New head of reversed list
}
```

Node Representation:
Each term in a polynomial P(x) = a_n * x^e_n + ... + a_0 * x^e_0 is stored in a linked list node containing coefficient, exponent, and next pointer:
```c
struct PolyNode {
    int coeff;
    int exp;
    struct PolyNode *next;
};
```
Nodes are maintained in strictly descending order of exponents.

Doubly Linked List (DLL):
- Node Structure: Each node contains three fields: `LLINK` (`prev`), `DATA`, and `RLINK` (`next`).
  ```c
  struct DNode {
      struct DNode *prev;
      int data;
      struct DNode *next;
  };
  ```
- Advantage: Allows bidirectional traversal (forward and backward). Deletion of a given node pointer takes O(1) time without traversing from head.
- DLL Insertion at Beginning: `newNode->next = head; newNode->prev = NULL; if (head != NULL) head->prev = newNode; head = newNode;`
- DLL Deletion of Given Node `ptr`: `if (ptr->prev != NULL) ptr->prev->next = ptr->next; else head = ptr->next; if (ptr->next != NULL) ptr->next->prev = ptr->prev; free(ptr);`

