key_points:
Dynamic Method Dispatch (Runtime Polymorphism - KTU Core Question):
- Mechanism by which a call to an overridden method is resolved at RUNTIME rather than compile time.
- Principle: A superclass reference variable can point to any subclass object:
  `SuperClass ref = new SubClass();`
- When an overridden method is invoked via `ref.show()`, the JVM determines which implementation to execute based upon the TYPE OF THE ACTUAL OBJECT referenced at runtime, NOT the type of the reference variable!
- Java Code Demonstration:
```java
class Shape { void draw() { System.out.println("Drawing Shape"); } }
class Circle extends Shape { void draw() { System.out.println("Drawing Circle"); } }
class Square extends Shape { void draw() { System.out.println("Drawing Square"); } }
public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape s; // Superclass reference
        s = new Circle(); s.draw(); // Prints "Drawing Circle"
        s = new Square(); s.draw(); // Prints "Drawing Square"
    }
}
```

pyqs.sol:
Dynamic Method Dispatch is the mechanism by which a call to an overridden method is resolved at RUNTIME based on the actual object instance referenced, rather than the reference type.
Program:
```java
class Bank {
    float getRate() { return 0.0f; }
}
class SBI extends Bank {
    float getRate() { return 7.5f; }
}
class HDFC extends Bank {
    float getRate() { return 8.2f; }
}
public class TestBank {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bank b; // Superclass reference variable
        b = new SBI();
        System.out.println("SBI Rate: " + b.getRate()); // Prints 7.5
        b = new HDFC();
        System.out.println("HDFC Rate: " + b.getRate()); // Prints 8.2
    }
}
```
Explanation: Even though reference `b` is of type `Bank`, the method executed is determined dynamically at runtime by the object instantiated (`new SBI()` or `new HDFC()`), achieving true runtime polymorphism.

